§14.3's bundling claim, and the experiment it cites

  the paragraph's evidence: "80/80 agreement, bundled and decomposed" — a test in which bundling changed NOTHING
  the paragraph's conclusion: "the theorem survives, but only when the presentation
  respects it" — which requires a case in which bundling changes SOMETHING.

  A three-cell counterexample on the 2×2×2 box: [(0, 0, 0), (0, 1, 1), (1, 0, 1)]
    presented decomposed, three chain coordinates:  E = 1
    presented bundled, coordinates 1 and 2 folded:  E = 0
    the cell the decomposed closure adds and the bundled one cannot see: [(0, 0, 1)]

  So the claim is TRUE and the cited experiment does not show it.

  The reason is general, and it is one line: with two coordinates the only pairwise
  projection is the set itself, so R(X) = X for EVERY X. Any presentation bundled down
  to two coordinates reports closure whatever it contains. Bundling does not hide a
  defect by accident; at two coordinates it cannot do anything else.

  RECORDED, NOT REPAIRED. The chapter is right about the mechanism; the sentence that
  supports it needs an experiment that exhibits it, and one is printed above.
